    </h1>
    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p>My research asks how people learn, how AI systems arrive at their behaviour, and what changes when people think and work with AI. I study how experience becomes knowledge, how that knowledge carries into new situations, what AI models learn internally, and how AI tools shape attention, practice, understanding, memory, and judgment.</p>
      <transition name="research-details">
        <div v-if="introExpanded" class="research-details">
          <p>I study human-AI interaction as a cognitive and design problem. As AI enters learning, writing, work, and decision-making, parts of the cognitive work can move between the person and the tool. I want to understand how that changes what people attend to, practise, understand, remember, and decide, and how systems can be designed to support the development of their own knowledge and judgment.</p>
          <p>On the human side, I study how people learn, represent, and use knowledge. I use experiments and computational models to ask how experience changes internal representations, how those representations support memory, concepts, generalisation, reasoning, and action, and how knowledge continues to change as it is used in new situations. Some of this work is conducted as a part-time research assistant at HKU's <a href="https://slrlab.edu.hku.hk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SLR Lab</a>, with Dr. Stephen M. K. Lee.</p>
          <p>On the AI side, I study what models learn and how that learning gives rise to behaviour. I look at what information is represented internally, how it is used during a task, and how internal computation produces the capabilities and failures we observe. I use behavioural tests and causal interventions when they help answer those questions.</p>
          <p>The three areas are closely connected in my work. Cognitive models give me ways to think about how knowledge can be represented and used, while AI models provide another kind of learned system in which those questions can be studied. Human-AI interaction brings the two together because the model becomes part of the environment in which a person learns, reasons, writes, and decides.</p>
