        <SearchPalette
          embedded
          fixed-mode="search"
          initial-query="explore"
        />
        <p>
          A query such as HSBC can rely heavily on exact wording because the same name appears in the source material.
          A question about how learning connects different parts of my work is harder because the most relevant
          passages may express the idea without using the words in the question.
        </p>
        <p>
          Search therefore uses both lexical and semantic retrieval. Lexical retrieval looks for matching words and is
          useful when the wording itself matters, especially for names, titles, places, and technical terms. Semantic
          retrieval compares meaning, allowing a relevant passage to appear even when it uses different language.
        </p>
        <p>
          Semantic retrieval relies on embeddings. Each passage is represented numerically from its text, so passages
          with related meanings tend to have embeddings that are closer together. The query can be represented in the
          same way and compared with material across the site. Search combines this semantic comparison with lexical
          matching so that exact terms remain strong while related ideas can still appear.
        </p>
        <div id="retrieval-before-generation" class="interactive-surface retrieval-surface" data-scroll-pop="true">
          <div class="surface-heading">
            <span>Compare word and meaning matches</span>
            <p>Try the same query across lexical and semantic retrieval and see what each one finds before the two rankings are combined.</p>
          </div>
          <RetrievalLab />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="90">
        <div class="impl-details">
          <button
