          <div id="search-details-panel" v-show="searchDetailsOpen" class="impl-details-panel">
            <p>
              When the site is built, its public content is divided into searchable passages and embedded with
              all-MiniLM-L6-v2, a compact sentence-embedding model. The resulting passage embeddings are prepared in
              advance and shipped with the site.
            </p>
            <p>
              When someone searches, the browser uses the same model to embed the query. Semantic retrieval compares
              that query embedding with the stored passage embeddings using cosine similarity, which measures how
              closely the vectors align. Lexical retrieval runs alongside it using a TF-IDF-like score, which gives
              more weight to words that are informative across the site than to words that appear almost everywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
              Weighted reciprocal-rank fusion combines the two rankings according to where each passage appears in
              them rather than trying to compare the two scoring systems directly. Lexical results receive additional
              weight so that a precise name or phrase can remain prominent when semantic retrieval also finds broader
              matches.
            </p>
            <p>
              Search also applies a minimum semantic similarity before keeping a result. A ranking can always place
              something first even when very little on the site is relevant, so the threshold removes weak matches
              instead of filling the result list with increasingly distant material.
            </p>
            <p>
              The stored embeddings are quantised into 8-bit integers together with the scale needed for comparison,
              which reduces the amount of data the site needs to load. Because the passage embeddings are already
              prepared, only the visitor&rsquo;s query has to be embedded at search time, and retrieval can run directly
              in the browser.
            </p>
